Test spatial identity using proper rotations and ligand labels (@chemistry-spatial-identity: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Name-a-configuration-without-mistaking-the-label-for-a-measurement.lingua#r_0010477871934A2121590BEFBD") { r_00978F6428EF14A811F9486FF4
To compare two rigid spatial models, allow a translation and a proper rotation of the whole object, together with correspondence between chemically equivalent atoms. A proper rotation preserves distances and orientation. Reflection also preserves distances, but reverses orientation; it is not a motion obtained by turning a rigid object in three-dimensional space. A chiral spatial structure cannot be superposed on its mirror by the permitted proper motions and equivalent-atom correspondences. Read [Organic Chemistry tenth edition by John McMurry](../sources/Organic-Chemistry-tenth-edition-by-John-McMurry.lingua#r_00F02946DB1C7E37631D69129A), sections 5.1–5.2, while keeping the molecular identity and conformational questions distinct.

Introduce orientation explicitly. For four ordered points p₁,p₂,p₃,p₄ define D=(p₁−p₄)·[(p₂−p₄)×(p₃−p₄)]. The cross product comes from [Describe orientation angular motion and the acceleration of a rotating point](../../physics/core/Describe-orientation-angular-motion-and-the-acceleration-of-a-rotating-point.lingua#r_00A972EA7F9A9AF87E148752A8) and the dot product from shared coordinate geometry. Expanding components gives the three-by-three determinant: for column vectors a,b,c it is a_x(b_yc_z−b_zc_y)−b_x(a_yc_z−a_zc_y)+c_x(a_yb_z−a_zb_y). D is six times a signed tetrahedral volume. Translation cancels from each difference; exchanging any two ordered vertices reverses the sign; D=0 for coplanar vertices. The sign requires a fixed right-handed coordinate convention and a declared order of labels.

For a matrix Q preserving the Euclidean dot product, QᵀQ=Id. Component expansion of the determinant gives det(QA)=det(Q)det(A), so det(Q)²=1. The two possibilities are +1 and −1. Proper rotations have +1 and preserve D; reflection diag(−1,1,1) has −1 and reverses it. A continuous path of orthogonal matrices starting at the identity cannot change determinant sign without leaving those possibilities. This proves why an orientation-reversing reflection cannot be supplied by continuous rigid turning. The full theory of rotations is later mathematics; these component identities suffice for this comparison.

Use the original regular tetrahedron v₁=(1,1,1), v₂=(1,−1,−1), v₃=(−1,1,−1), v₄=(−1,−1,1). Its center is zero, every squared edge length is eight and D=16. Its mirror has the same six edge lengths but D=−16 for the same ordered labels. Thus pairwise distances alone cannot distinguish the handedness of four distinct labels. D is not a general molecular chirality detector: label order must be meaningful, a coplanar quadruple may be uninformative within a larger chiral structure, and repeated equivalent ligands permit additional correspondences.

There are 24 permutations of the four tetrahedral vertices. Each preserves their mutual distances and is realized by a unique orthogonal transformation of this centered regular tetrahedron: any three vertices form a basis, their Gram products are preserved, and the fourth is minus their sum. Swapping two vertices changes D's sign. Pairing every permutation with one fixed swap gives twelve sign-preserving and twelve sign-reversing permutations. The twelve even permutations are precisely the proper rotations of this tetrahedron; the other twelve include reflection.

Assign four distinct ligand labels A,B,C,D to the four sites. The 24 assignments divide into two proper-rotation classes of twelve each, exchanged by reflection. If at least two site labels are identical in this point-ligand model, swapping those identical labels leaves the physical labeling unchanged while reversing permutation parity. Any improper correspondence can then be composed with that swap to make a proper one, so every assignment of that multiset lies in one rotation class. The patterns ABCD, AABC, AABB, AAAB and AAAA therefore give class counts 2,1,1,1,1. This conclusion is for a single tetrahedral point-ligand arrangement; a label hiding an internally chiral or flexible group needs its full chemical meaning.

**Work:** calculate D from components, reflect the points and verify the distance/sign contrast. Prove the repeated-label argument rather than simply dividing every assignment count by twelve. Relate the four-distinct-group center in the book's 2-butanol example to the abstract model, while keeping the actual atomic geometry distinct from the regular tetrahedron. A tetrahedral chirality center is one source of molecular handedness, not its universal definition.

**Continue:** [Name a configuration without mistaking the label for a measurement](Name-a-configuration-without-mistaking-the-label-for-a-measurement.lingua#r_0010477871934A2121590BEFBD).

